    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **272 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented telephone normalization so numbers are stored digits-only, returned as stored, and normalized before the uniqueness check.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Relaxed the `telephone` validation pattern from `^[0-9]*$` to `^[0-9 ()-]*$`. Without this, a formatted number like `(613) 555-0100` would fail `@Valid` binding and return 400 before any normalization could run. (The regenerated `OwnerFieldsDto` picks this up on build.)\n\n2. **`.../function/owner/TelephoneNormalizer.java`** (new) \u2014 Small utility with `digitsOnly(String)` that strips all non-digit characters (spaces, dashes, parentheses, etc.), null-safe.\n\n3. **`BuildOwner.java`** (POST/create) \u2014 Normalizes the mapped owner's telephone to digits-only immediately after mapping, *before* `RejectDuplicateOwner` runs. So the stored value is digits-only and the uniqueness comparison sees the normalized form \u2014 `(613) 555-0100` and `6135550100` collide and the second is rejected 409.\n\n4. **`ApplyOwner.java`** (PUT/update) \u2014 Same normalization applied on update, keeping stored telephones consistently digits-only.\n\nThe existing `RejectDuplicateOwner` / `DuplicateMatching` comparison then operates on already-normalized values, so the uniqueness check works correctly. Both new Cp11 tests (`coreStoresDigitsOnly`, `functionalityNormalisedDuplicateRejected`) pass, and no existing tests regressed.",
